+# Fix and review pull requests from one loop
+
+One loop per session fixes and reviews its pull requests, polls each one
+once, and spends GraphQL only on a pull request that changed.
+
+## 1. Problem
+
+Projector drives a pull request to a clean review with two loops. Each is a
+skill that keeps a watcher script running and starts one subagent per pull
+request. The operator is the GitHub account `gh` is signed in as.
+
+- **The watch loop**, `start-watch-loop`, runs once per session, in the
+  top-level conversation. Its scope is a tracked file of `owner/repo#number`
+  lines: the pull requests that conversation created or adopted. Its watcher,
+  `skills/start-watch-loop/scripts/watch-threads.sh`, reports `FINDING`,
+  `VERDICT`, `CONFLICT`, `REVIEW`, `DRAFT`, `TRACKED`, and `DONE`, and a
+  fixer subagent, `fix-<n>` or `fix-stack-<n>`, runs `fix-pr` on the pull
+  request.
+- **The review loop**, `start-review-loop`, runs once per repository. It
+  tracks every open pull request the operator wrote and every one that
+  requests the operator's review. Its watcher,
+  `skills/start-review-loop/scripts/watch-prs.sh`, reports `NEW PR`,
+  `NEW HEAD`, `RESPONDED`, and `CLOSED`, and a reviewer subagent,
+  `review-<n>`, runs `review-pr` on each new head. A pull request stays in
+  draft until its head reviews clean.
+
+Two problems follow from running them apart:
+
+1. **Each pull request is polled twice.** A pull request that a session
+   opens is in both loops' sets, and each watcher queries it on its own
+   schedule.
+   Projector's panel, which lists the loops' pull requests above the prompt,
+   queries them a third time.
+2. **Every pass spends GraphQL whether or not anything changed.** The watch
+   loop spends 1 GraphQL point per pull request per pass, plus a REST call
+   for its reviews. The review loop spends 2 points per pull request per
+   pass, plus 2 to 4 points to find new pull requests. GitHub gives an
+   account 5,000 GraphQL points an hour, shared by every session, loop, and
+   machine signed in as that account. At the default 60-second interval with
+   10 pull requests, the two loops spend about 2,000 points an hour, and
+   each further review loop, for another model or on another machine, adds
+   about 1,400. When the budget runs out, every query fails until the hour
+   resets, and a watcher carries its last answer forward, so no new finding
+   surfaces. The limit is reached in practice: one session slowed its
+   watcher to a 10-minute interval to cope, so findings took up to 10
+   minutes to surface.
+
+A single loop per session must also decide who reviews. The review loop
+runs once per repository because two loops reviewing as one model under one
+account duplicate every review. Today nothing but the user's care keeps a
+second one from starting, and a second machine already collides this way.
+
+## 2. Solution
+
+One skill, `start-pr-loop`, replaces both loops, and it runs one loop per
+session through a new CLI command, `project loop watch`. The loop's tracked
+file gives each pull request one or both of two roles: `fix` for a pull
+request this conversation owns, and `review` for the operator's pull
+requests and those that request the operator's review. Each pass asks GitHub
+a conditional REST question per pull request, which costs nothing when
+GitHub answers `304 Not Modified`. The loop runs the GraphQL query only for
+a pull request whose answer changed, and for every pull request in a full
+pass every 10 minutes, which covers what no conditional request sees. It
+dispatches each event by role: a new head goes to the pull request's
+reviewer, and a finding, verdict, conflict, or review body goes to its
+fixer. One loop per repository and model holds the review role, through a
+lease that the operating system releases when the holder's watcher ends.
+Every other loop only fixes, and the next one to pass takes the lease and
+the holder's review lines. Loops on one machine share their ETags and query
+results, so a change costs one query however many sessions track the pull
+request.
+
+### 2.1 One pass
+
+1. **Read the tracked file.** The loop re-reads its roles and exclusions
+   every pass, as both watchers do today, and announces a bad line once as
+   `TRACKED`.
+2. **Settle the review role.** Unless review is off for this loop, it tries
+   the lease for its repository and model as § 2.4 describes. It prints
+   `ROLE` when it takes the lease, when it finds another loop holding it on
+   the first pass, and when it loses it.
+3. **Adopt.** The holder sends one conditional request per page of the
+   repository's open pull requests. On a changed page, it appends
+   `owner/repo#number review` for each open pull request that the operator
+   wrote or that requests the operator by login, unless the file already
+   lists it for review or keeps it out of review. A failed request adopts
+   nothing that pass.
+4. **Ask the conditional questions.** For each tracked pull request, the
+   loop sends a conditional request for the pull request and for the page
+   its next review lands on. For each base branch of a pull request it
+   fixes, it sends one for the branch's ref. § 2.2 lists the requests.
+5. **Pick the dirty pull requests.** A pull request is dirty when one of
+   its requests answered `200` or failed, when its base moved and the loop
+   fixes it, when its last result left `mergeable` at `UNKNOWN`, when it has
+   no result yet, or when its result is older than `--full-every`. When
+   another loop on the machine queried a clean pull request since this loop
+   last read it, this loop reads that cached result and queries nothing.
+6. **Query each dirty pull request.** The loop runs one GraphQL query, and
+   reads the REST reviews when the reviews page changed or the full pass is
+   due. It writes the new ETags and the result to the machine's cache. A
+   failed query carries the pull request's rows forward, as both watchers
+   do today.
+7. **Announce by role.** The loop works out what is outstanding on each
+   pull request from its result, for each role it holds, and prints each new
+   event and each reminder that is due, as § 2.5 lists.
+8. **Write state and sleep.** The loop writes its `--state` file, checks
+   `--exit-when-closed`, and sleeps `--interval` seconds.
+
+The loop stays level-triggered. The conditional requests only decide which
+pull requests to query sooner. Every event is still worked out from what is
+outstanding, every pull request still gets a full query within
+`--full-every`, and a request that fails leads to the query rather than to
+silence.
+
+### 2.2 The conditional requests
+
+Each request sends the `ETag` its resource last answered with in an
+`If-None-Match` header. GitHub documents that a `304` answer does not count
+against the primary rate limit, and in a probe three `304`s in a row left
+`X-Ratelimit-Used` unchanged. Observed means seen in a probe of two open
+pull requests in this repository. Inferred means read from the fields the
+response carries, on the assumption that the `ETag` hashes the body, and not
+yet tested by triggering the event.
+
+| Request | Sent | Its `ETag` moves on | Status |
+| --- | --- | --- | --- |
+| `GET repos/{owner}/{repo}/pulls/{n}` | Per tracked pull request | A push (`head.sha`), a draft toggle (`draft`), a merge or close (`state`, `merged`), an issue comment such as a hold (`comments`, `updated_at`), an inline comment or reply (`review_comments`), and a change in mergeability or CI (`mergeable`, `mergeable_state`) | The fields are observed. An issue comment moving `updated_at` is observed. The rest is inferred. |
+| `GET repos/{owner}/{repo}/pulls/{n}/reviews?per_page=100&page=<p>`, where `<p>` is `floor(R / 100) + 1` for `R` reviews | Per tracked pull request | A new review, including a body-only `COMMENT` review, which no count on the pull request shows | Inferred |
+| `GET repos/{owner}/{repo}/git/ref/heads/{base}` | Per base branch of a pull request the loop fixes | A push to the base, which can bring a conflict while the head stands still | Inferred |
+| `GET repos/{owner}/{repo}/pulls?state=open&per_page=100`, each page | Per repository, by the review role's holder | A new pull request, a review request (`requested_reviewers`), and a push or draft toggle on any open pull request | The fields are observed. |
+
+No request covers a thread that is resolved or unresolved without a reply
+(inferred). A `RESPONDED` that comes from the author resolving every thread
+without pushing, and a `FINDING` that comes from a reopened thread, wait for
+the full pass unless a reply arrives with them. A reply moves
+`review_comments`, and `fix-pr` always replies before it resolves.
+
+`gh api` reports a conditional answer only in the status line that `-i`
+prints. It exits `1` on a `304`, as it does on a `404`, a server error, and
+a network failure, and with `--jq` a `304` prints
+`unexpected end of JSON input`. So the loop runs
+`gh api -i -H 'If-None-Match: <etag>' <path>`, reads the status line, the
+headers, and then the body, and never relies on the exit status.
+
+| Status line | The loop |
+| --- | --- |
+| `200` | Stores the new `ETag` and marks the pull request dirty. For the open-pulls list, it adopts from the body. |
+| `304 Not Modified` | Changes nothing. |
+| `404` for a pull request | Announces `TRACKED … no such repository or pull request` once, as today. |
+| `404` for a base ref | Treats the base as gone, usually because the layer below merged and its branch was deleted. The loop queries the pull request once, which reads its new base, and asks for that base's ref from then on. |
+| Any other status, or none | Treats the request as failed and marks the pull request dirty, so the GraphQL query runs. |
+
+The GraphQL query is the union of the two queries the watchers run today,
+so one result serves both roles:
+
+```graphql
+repository(owner: $o, name: $r) { visibility
+  pullRequest(number: $n) {
+    state isDraft reviewDecision mergeable headRefOid headRefName baseRefName author { login }
+    comments(last: 100) { nodes { author { login } body } }
+    reviews(last: 100) { nodes { author { login } authorAssociation submittedAt body } }
+    changesRequested: reviews(states: [CHANGES_REQUESTED], last: 1) { nodes { author { login } body commit { oid } } }
+    reviewThreads(first: 100) { nodes { id isResolved path line
+      first: comments(first: 1) { nodes { databaseId body } }
+      last: comments(last: 1) { nodes { databaseId author { login } body } } } } } }
+```
+
+### 2.3 The tracked file
+
+A tracked file lists each pull request with its roles:
+
+```text
+acme/app#12
+acme/app#12 review
+acme/app#14 review
+!acme/app#9
+!acme/app#15 review
+```
+
+- **`owner/repo#number`** lists the pull request in the `fix` role, so a
+  file written before roles reads as it always has. `owner/repo#number fix`
+  means the same.
+- **`owner/repo#number review`** lists it in the `review` role.
+- **Several lines for one pull request** give it the union of their roles,
+  and one line may name both, as `acme/app#12 fix review`.
+- **`!` before any of these** keeps the pull request out of the roles the
+  line names, wherever the line sits. `!acme/app#9` keeps it out of `fix`,
+  as the same line keeps it out of a watch loop today, and
+  `!acme/app#15 review` keeps it out of `review` alone. A session that stops
+  fixing a pull request never stops another session from reviewing it, and
+  the other way round.
+
+References match without regard to case, as today. Only the CLI writes the
+file. `project loop add` and `drop` write a role-less line for `fix`, so a
+watcher from an earlier release, which reports any other shape as `TRACKED`,
+still reads a file that `implement` adds to. Only the new loop's review
+lines carry a word.
+
+### 2.4 The review lease
+
+One loop per repository, reviewer account, and model holds the review role.
+A Claude Code loop and a Codex loop on different models each hold their own
+lease and give separate opinions, as review loops on several models do
+today. Two sessions on one model share one lease.
+
+The lease is two files in
+`$XDG_STATE_HOME/projector/loops/leases/<owner>/<repo>/<reviewer>/`, where
+`<model-key>` is the folder name `project review` derives from the model ID:
+`<model-key>.lock`, which the holder locks, and `<model-key>.json`, which
+names the holder.
+
+| `.json` field | Value |
+| --- | --- |
+| `tracked` | The holder's tracked file, resolved, which identifies the holder loop |
+| `host`, `pid` | Where the holder's watcher runs |
+| `model`, `reviewer` | The model ID and the login the review role posts as |
+| `generation` | How many times the lease has changed hands. Each take by another loop raises it by one, and a holder that takes its own lease back keeps it. |
+| `loop` | The holder's loop id, `<repo>-review-<model-key>-<generation>`, which its reviewers pass to `project review` as `--loop` |
+| `taken_at`, `renewed_at` | When the holder took the lease, and when it last passed |
+
+- **Holding.** The holder keeps an exclusive lock on the `.lock` file for
+  as long as its watcher runs, through the portable `try_lock` the inbox
+  uses. It rewrites `renewed_at` each pass, and once more when it exits on
+  a signal it can catch. The operating system drops the lock when the
+  process ends, however it ends, so a session that closes passes the lease
+  on without writing anything. A process keeps its locks while the machine
+  sleeps, so waking from sleep moves no lease.
+- **Taking.** Every other loop tries the lock once a pass, without
+  waiting. When it gets the lock, it keeps it if the `.json` is missing,
+  names this loop's own tracked file, or holds a `renewed_at` older than 5
+  minutes. Otherwise it lets the lock go. The 5 minutes let a holder whose
+  watcher restarts, as a host's monitor restarts a command that expires,
+  take its lease back before another session does.
+- **Handing over.** On taking the lease, a loop reads the tracked file the
+  `.json` names. It copies every review line and every review exclusion,
+  such as `!acme/app#15 review`, from that file into its own, whatever this
+  loop fixes. A review the user assigned and a dismissal from review both
+  survive the handover, and an exclusion from `fix` stays with the session
+  that wrote it. It also copies the departed loop id's record of published
+  reviews into its own, so an incremental review can still build on a full
+  review the departed holder published. Then the loop writes the `.json`
+  with the next `generation` and its own loop id, prints `ROLE`, and adopts.
+  A pull request that is new to this loop reports `NEW PR` unless its head
+  already has a verdict by this model from the reviewer's account, as
+  `watch-prs.sh` decides today, so a handover reviews no head twice.
+- **Reviews in flight.** A review's state, start comment, and lock belong
+  to the machine and the model, under
+  `prs/<owner>/<repo>/<number>/<model-key>/` in the review state directory,
+  not to a session. So a review the departed holder left unpublished passes
+  to the taker with the role, and its reviewer may still be running if only
+  the departed watcher stopped. One verdict at most lands on the head:
+  1. On the pass that takes the lease, the watcher finds each head whose
+     lock names an earlier loop id of this role and has no verdict, and
+     marks it on its `NEW PR` line.
+  2. The taker's reviewer runs `project review setup --take-over`. It
+     refuses when the head already has a verdict by this model, and the
+     reviewer stops. Otherwise it rewrites the lock to name the taker's loop
+     id and sets the review up in the departed one's place, replacing its
+     start comment because this machine's state records it.
+  3. `publish`, `move`, and `settle` refuse when the head's lock names
+     another loop id, and say that another loop took the review over. The
+     departed reviewer stops and reports there, and never runs `setup`
+     again.
+  4. If the departed reviewer's `publish` passed its lock check before the
+     take-over and submits after it, its verdict is not in the taker's
+     record. So the taker's `publish` refuses and asks the user, through the
+     branch of the collision check that a different loop id reaches.
+- **Losing.** A loop with review lines that finds the `.json` naming
+  another loop's tracked file removes the review role from its lines and
+  prints `ROLE`. Its conversation stops its reviewer subagents and runs no
+  `project review finish`, which would delete the review state that now
+  belongs to the taker. Only the `.json` decides the loss. A restarted
+  holder that finds the lock taken for a moment, by a loop that then lets
+  it go, keeps its review lines and tries again next pass.
+- **Staying out.** `--no-review`, or `loop.review = false`, keeps a loop
+  away from the lease entirely.
+
+The lock is local, so two machines signed in as one account can each hold
+a lease for one repository and model. The per-head guards that exist today
+catch the duplicate. `project review setup` refuses while another session's
+live start comment shows the same model reviewing the pull request, on this
+machine or another. `project review publish` refuses a second verdict by
+the same model on one head that this loop's record does not hold. The skill
+reads the first such refusal as a second machine reviewing, tells the user
+once, and names the remedy: run
+`project config set loop.review false --user` on the machine that is to
+only fix.
+
+### 2.5 Events and where they go
+
+Every event both watchers print today keeps its wording and meaning. The
+loop prints an event only for a role the pull request holds.
+
+| Event | Role | Goes to |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| `FINDING`, `VERDICT`, `CONFLICT`, `REVIEW` | `fix` | The pull request's fixer, `fix-<n>`, or `fix-stack-<n>` for a stack |
+| `DRAFT` | `fix`, only when the loop does not also review the pull request | Nobody. It reports that no review has signed off the head, and the loop's own reviewer already covers a pull request it reviews. |
+| `NEW PR`, `NEW HEAD`, `RESPONDED` | `review` | The pull request's reviewer, `review-<n>` |
+| `BRANCH` | `review`, with `--worktree` | The main loop, which looks for an open pull request on the branch |
+| `CLOSED` | Any | The main loop, which removes the pull request and finishes its subagents |
+| `TRACKED` | Any | The main loop, which fixes the file |
+| `ROLE` | `review` | The main loop, which says whether this session reviews, and stops its reviewers without `project review finish` when it does not |
+| `DONE` | `fix`, with `--exit-when-closed` | The main loop, which ends the watch |
+
+`DONE` means that every pull request the loop fixes has merged or closed
+and that the loop does not hold the review lease. A loop that holds the
+lease keeps running, because it reviews for the whole repository. As today,
+a pass with a failed query or a bad line never reports `DONE`.
+
+`ROLE` names the repository, the model, and what changed:
+
+```text
+ROLE acme/app review taken for claude-opus-5-5 as loop app-review-claude-opus-5-5-3 — this loop reviews the repository's pull requests
+ROLE acme/app review held for claude-opus-5-5 by host-a pid 4182 (~/loops/a/tracked) — this loop only fixes
+ROLE acme/app review lost for claude-opus-5-5 to host-a pid 4182 (~/loops/a/tracked) — stop this loop's reviewers, and leave their review state to that loop
+```
+
+### 2.6 Commands and settings
+
+`project loop watch` runs the loop and prints one event a line, as
+`project inbox watch` prints answers:
+
+| Option | Default | Meaning |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| `--tracked <file>` | Required | The loop's tracked file |
+| `--state <file>` | Required | The loop's own record: what it announced and when, the head it last saw for review, and which cached result it last read for each pull request |
+| `--model <id>` | Required | The session's model ID. It keys the lease and recognizes a head this model already reviewed. |
+| `--repo <owner/repo>` | This checkout's origin | The repository whose review role the loop may hold and whose pull requests it adopts |
+| `--reviewer <login>` | `review.username`, then the operator | The account the review role posts as |
+| `--no-review` | Off | Fix only |
+| `--interval <seconds>` | `60`, at least `30` | Time between passes |
+| `--full-every <seconds>` | `600` | The longest a pull request goes without a GraphQL query |
+| `--renotify <seconds>` | `900` | When an outstanding finding, verdict, conflict, or draft is announced again |
+| `--worktree <dir>` | None | The checkout whose branch changes `BRANCH` reports |
+| `--once` | Off | Make one pass and exit |
+| `--exit-when-closed` | Off | End with `DONE`, as § 2.5 defines it |
+
+The other commands change as follows:
+
+- **`project loop add <ref> --tracked <file> [--role fix|review]`** appends
+  a line for the role, `fix` by default, unless the file lists the pull
+  request in that role already. It still refuses a pull request the file
+  keeps out of that role.
+- **`project loop drop` and `restore`** take the same `--role`, `fix` by
+  default. `drop` appends an exclusion for the role, and `restore` removes
+  the role's exclusions.
+- **`project loop remove <ref> --tracked <file> [--role fix|review]`**
+  removes only that role with `--role`, and every line that lists the pull
+  request without it. It leaves every exclusion, as today.
+- **`project loop lease <owner/repo> [--model <id>] [--json]`** prints each
+  review lease of the repository: its model, reviewer, holder, loop id,
+  tracked file, `renewed_at`, and whether a watcher holds its lock now. It
+  writes nothing.
+- **`loop.review`** is a new boolean setting, `true` by default. `false`
+  keeps every loop it applies to out of the review role.
+
+Loops on one machine share a cache under
+`$XDG_STATE_HOME/projector/loops/cache/<operator>/<owner>/<repo>/`: one file
+per pull request with each request's last `ETag` and the last GraphQL result
+with the time it was fetched, one per base branch, and one per page of the
+open-pulls list. The cache is keyed by the operator because GitHub varies an
+`ETag` with the token. Each write replaces the file whole, and two loops
+that race on one pull request at worst both query it.
+
+### 2.7 The skill and its callers
+
+`start-pr-loop` holds what both loop skills hold today, minus what the CLI
+now does:
+
+- **Scope.** The skill runs only in the top-level conversation. It adds each
+  pull request the conversation creates or adopts with `project loop add`,
+  keeps the tracked and state files beside each other outside any checkout,
+  and records their paths in a persistent recurring goal.
+- **Start.** It resolves the operator, the reviewer, and the model, runs one
+  pass with `--once`, dispatches that pass's events, and then runs the
+  watcher under the host's persistent process or monitor facility.
+- **Review role.** The first time the loop takes the role, the skill runs
+  `project review rules` once and warns as the review loop does today. Every
+  reviewer passes the loop id that the `ROLE` line and the lease name to
+  `project review` as `--loop`. Each holding of the lease has its own id,
+  so one loop writes each record and the collision check tells two holders
+  apart. A reviewer whose `NEW PR` line marks a review left in flight runs
+  `setup --take-over`, and a reviewer told that another loop took its
+  review over stops and reports, as § 2.4 describes.
+- **Subagents.** A fix-role pull request gets one fixer and a review-role
+  pull request one reviewer, named and briefed as the two skills do today,
+  each in its own worktree. A pull request in both roles has both.
+- **Assigned reviews.** To review a pull request the user names, the skill
+  adds it with `--role review` to its own file when it holds the lease, and
+  otherwise to the holder's file, which `project loop lease --json` names.
+- **Reports.** The skill reports clean state and stack readiness as
+  `start-watch-loop` does today. On `CLOSED` it removes the pull request,
+  finishes its fixer, and runs `project review finish --force` for its
+  reviewer. On a `ROLE` line that loses the role, it stops its reviewers
+  and finishes no review.
+
+`implement` starts `start-pr-loop` with `--exit-when-closed` on the pull
+request it opens, or adds the pull request to the loop the conversation
+already runs. Its rule to leave reviewing to a separate loop goes, because
+its loop takes the review role itself when no other loop on the machine
+holds it. `fix-pr` and `review-pr` change only where they name
+the loop that runs them.
+
+### 2.8 The panel
+
+The panel learns about a loop from the command that starts its watcher, and
+lists the pull requests in its tracked file.
+
+- **Recognize the new command.** `project loop watch … --tracked <file>` in
+  a Bash or Monitor call records a loop of kind `loop`, as `watch-threads.sh`
+  and `watch-prs.sh` record `watching` and `reviewing` today.
+- **Read roles.** A role-less line means `fix` in a `loop` or `watching`
+  file and `review` in a `reviewing` file, so a loop that an earlier watcher
+  started lists correctly until its session ends.
+- **Name the roles.** A pull request's drawer says `fixing` in the blue
+  that `watching` uses today, `reviewing` in `claude` orange, or both.
+- **Dismiss by role.** Dismissing a pull request runs `project loop drop`
+  with `--role` for each role a `loop` file lists it in, so the session
+  stops only what it does. In a file an earlier watcher reads, the panel
+  writes the role-less line that watcher understands, as today.
+
+### 2.9 Both hosts
+
+A loop needs only the CLI and the shared skill. The panel, which only Claude
+Code draws, reads what the CLI writes, and nothing depends on MCP. Codex runs
+the same `project loop watch` under its own process facility, and its model
+ID gives it a lease of its own.
+
+### 2.10 The files
+
+| File | Change |
+| --- | --- |
+| `src/projector/loop.py` | Roles in the tracked format, `add --role` and `remove --role`, and the review lease |
+| `src/projector/watch.py` (new) | The loop: passes, conditional requests, the machine cache, the GraphQL result, and events by role |
+| `src/projector/cli.py` | `loop watch`, `loop lease`, and `--role` |
+| `src/projector/config.py` | `loop.review` |
+| `src/projector/review.py` | `setup --take-over`, and `publish`, `move`, and `settle` refusing a head whose lock names another loop id |
+| `skills/start-pr-loop/SKILL.md` (new) | The one loop skill |
+| `skills/start-watch-loop/`, `skills/start-review-loop/` | Removed, with both watcher scripts |
+| `skills/implement/SKILL.md`, `skills/fix-pr/SKILL.md`, `skills/review-pr/SKILL.md` | Name `start-pr-loop`, drop "leave the review loop alone" from `implement`, and teach `review-pr` the take-over and the refusal it stops on |
