+
+## 3. Why this design
+
+- **One loop with roles, not two loops that share a cache.** A shared cache
+  would stop the double polling but keep two skills, two watchers with
+  different state shapes, and two subagent schemes for one pull request. A
+  role per pull request lets one pass serve both jobs, and lets a session
+  that owns a pull request and reviews it drive it to a clean head alone.
+- **A conditional request per pull request detects change.** One request
+  for the repository's open pull requests costs less, but it cannot see a
+  conflict, which shows only in `mergeable` on a single pull request, and
+  its `ETag` moves whenever any pull request in the repository changes. A
+  `304` per pull request is just as free and names the pull request that
+  changed. The list fits adoption, which needs every open pull request's
+  author and review requests, and it replaces the two `gh pr list` searches
+  that cost 2 to 4 GraphQL points every pass.
+- **Reviews and the base get requests of their own.** A body-only review
+  appears in no count on the pull request, so only the reviews resource
+  shows it for certain. Whether a conditional request makes GitHub
+  recompute mergeability is untested, so the base's ref tells the loop when
+  to look for a conflict. Rollout step 1 removes either request that a
+  measurement shows to be redundant.
+- **A full pass every 10 minutes.** It bounds the delay for what no request
+  sees, such as a thread resolved without a reply, at a cost of 12 points an
+  hour per pull request. It also catches any inference in § 2.2 that proves
+  wrong.
+- **The CLI, not a script in the skill.** Both plugins ship the skill tree,
+  and both hosts' skills already run the CLI, so either place reaches both
+  hosts. The CLI is the better place. It already parses the tracked file,
+  which today the two scripts parse again in bash. The watcher reuses
+  `verdict_marker`, `HOLD_MARKER`, `model_key`, `reviewer_login`, and
+  `authenticated_login` from `src/projector/review.py`, so the hold marker
+  that `project review hold` writes is the one the watcher reads by import
+  rather than by a copied pattern. The lease takes `try_lock` and `unlock`
+  from `src/projector/inbox.py`, which work on Windows as well as on POSIX.
+  The bash 3.2 that macOS ships, which the scripts must run on, has no
+  portable lock. `project inbox watch` is already a long-running CLI
+  watcher. The cost is version skew: a machine's CLI can lag its plugin.
+  The CLI and both plugins share one version, `project` warns when it is
+  behind, and the skill tells the user to run `project upgrade` when
+  `project loop watch` is missing.
+- **A loop id for each holding of the lease.** One id shared by every
+  holder makes a departed reviewer and the taker's reviewer one loop to
+  `project review`. The collision check then finds the first verdict in
+  "this loop's" record and points the second reviewer at `--rereview`, and
+  the missing lock points it at `setup`, so following both posts a second
+  verdict. A new id per holding lets the lock fence the departed reviewer
+  out and lets the collision check ask the user. Copying the departed
+  record at the handover keeps incremental reviews going across it.
+- **A lock, not an expiring lease file.** An expiring lease needs a clock,
+  so a laptop that wakes from sleep finds every lease expired and hands the
+  role to whichever session passes first. The operating system releases a
+  lock the moment its process ends, and keeps it while the process sleeps.
+  The `.json` beside the lock only names the holder and gives a restarting
+  holder 5 minutes to return.
+- **A local lease, not one on GitHub.** A lease on GitHub would need a
+  write on every pass to a place every machine can read. In a repository
+  that is a public mutation that needs write access, and a gist has no
+  compare-and-swap. The per-head guards already stop a second machine's
+  duplicate review before or as it publishes, so the local lease only has to
+  keep one machine's sessions apart, and `loop.review` lets the user settle
+  which machine reviews.
+- **Every loop competes for the review role.** Reviewing then never stops
+  while any session on the machine runs a loop, and the role passes on by
+  itself when a session ends. If only a loop the user starts could review,
+  reviewing would again depend on the user keeping a dedicated session
+  open. The cost is that the session that wrote a pull request can also
+  review it. A host's automatic approval has refused a reviewer subagent's
+  report to its main loop as self-approval, and a clean self-review's
+  `publish` needs the rules that `project review rules` checks for. The
+  skill warns once when those rules are missing, as the review loop does
+  today, and the open question in § 7 weighs this cost.
+- **Exclusions carry a role.** One exclusion for every role would let a
+  session that stops fixing a pull request also stop the repository's
+  review of it, and would make the handover choose between losing a review
+  dismissal and copying a fix dismissal. With a role on each exclusion, a
+  dismissal stops only what the dismissing session does, and the handover
+  copies review exclusions alone. The role-less form keeps the `fix`
+  meaning a watch loop's file gives it today.
+- **A cache shared across sessions.** The usual layout is one session
+  fixing a pull request and another reviewing it. Without the cache, both
+  query it on every change.
+- **What is replaced.** Both watcher scripts and both loop skills go. Every
+  caller moves to `start-pr-loop` or `project loop watch`: `implement`,
+  `fix-pr`, `review-pr`, the agent instructions template, the panel, the
+  docs, the homepage, and the tests that read the old files.
+- **The name `start-pr-loop`.** It follows the `start-*-loop` pattern and
+  the `-pr` set of `review-pr`, `fix-pr`, and `summarize-pr`. Claude Code
+  bundles a `/loop` skill, so a bare `loop` collides and `start-loop` reads
+  as a variant of it. No Claude Code 2.1.300 command or bundled skill is
+  named `start-pr-loop`.
+- **`watch.auto_start` keeps its name.** It still says whether `implement`
+  starts a loop, and renaming a setting would break every file that sets it.
+
+## 4. Acceptance criteria
+
+| Do | Expect |
+| --- | --- |
+| Run two passes over a tracked pull request whose requests answer `304` on the second | The second pass makes no `graphql` call |
+| Answer `200` for one pull request's request on the second pass | One GraphQL query, for that pull request |
+| Fail a request with a server error, a rate-limit `403`, or no status line | The GraphQL query runs for that pull request. When it fails too, the rows carry forward. |
+| Answer `404` for a pull request | `TRACKED … no such repository or pull request`, once |
+| Let `--full-every` pass while every request answers `304` | Each tracked pull request gets one query |
+| Leave `mergeable` at `UNKNOWN` | The pull request is queried each pass until it settles, and only `CONFLICTING` announces `CONFLICT` |
+| Move the base of a `fix` pull request and of a `review`-only one | Only the `fix` pull request is queried |
+| Give a `fix`, a `review`, and a two-role pull request an unresolved thread, a changes request, and a new head | `fix` prints `FINDING` and `VERDICT`, `review` prints `NEW HEAD`, the two-role one prints all three from one query and no `DRAFT` |
+| Start two watchers with different tracked files, one repository, reviewer, and model | The first prints `ROLE` taking the lease, the second prints `ROLE` naming the holder, and only the first adopts |
+| Repeat with two models | Each takes a lease |
+| Stop the first watcher, and pass the second more than 5 minutes after the lease's `renewed_at` | The second takes the lease and copies the first file's review lines and review exclusions, including those for pull requests the second fixes. A restarted first watcher prints `ROLE`, keeps no review line, and runs no `project review finish`. |
+| Leave a review lock on a head under the departed loop id, with no verdict, and hand the lease over | The taker's `NEW PR` line marks the head, and `setup --take-over` succeeds for it |
+| After that take-over, run the departed reviewer's `publish`, `move`, or `settle` with its own `--loop` | Each refuses, names the loop that took the review over, and says to stop rather than set up again. Only the taker's verdict lands. |
+| Publish the departed reviewer's verdict before the take-over | `setup --take-over` refuses because the head has a verdict by this model |
+| Let the departed reviewer's `publish` pass its lock check before the take-over and submit after it | The taker's `publish` refuses with the branch that asks the user, not the one that suggests `--rereview` |
+| Exclude the operator's pull request A from `fix` in the taker's file, and pull request B, which the taker fixes, from `review` in the holder's file, then hand the lease over | The taker reviews A but does not fix it, and fixes B but does not review it |
+| Restart the first watcher within 5 minutes | It takes its lease back, and the second stays fix-only |
+| Run with `--no-review`, or with `loop.review = false` | The lease files are never touched |
+| Track one pull request from two loops on one machine and change it | One GraphQL query in all |
+| Under `--exit-when-closed`, close every `fix` pull request, with and without the lease | `DONE` only without the lease |
+| Run `project loop add`, `add --role review`, and `remove --role review` | A role-less line, a `review` line, and the `review` word or line gone, the `fix` line kept |
+| Run `project loop drop --role review` on a pull request listed in both roles | The pull request leaves `review` and stays in `fix` |
+| Answer `404` for the base ref of a pull request whose base was deleted | One query reads the new base, and the next pass asks for the new base's ref |
+| Start `project loop watch --tracked <file>` in a Claude Code session | The panel lists each pull request with `fixing`, `reviewing`, or both, and still lists an earlier watcher's file by its kind |
+| Run the loop for an hour on a live repository with `N` quiet pull requests | `gh api rate_limit` shows the GraphQL count growing by about `12N` an hour, and not between full passes |
+
+These tests fail before the change: `tests/test_loop_watch.py`, the role
+and lease cases in `tests/test_loop.py`, the role and command cases in
+`hooks/parse.test.ts` and `hooks/panel.test.tsx`, and the skill set in
+`tests/test_packaging.py`.
+
+## 5. Cost
+
+The hot path is the pass, which runs every `--interval` seconds for as long
+as a session lives, in every session with a loop. Both budgets are per
+account: 5,000 GraphQL points and 5,000 counted REST requests an hour.
+
+Today, at the default 60-second interval, for `N` pull requests that both
+loops track, where `R` is a pull request's review count:
+
+| Consumer | GraphQL points an hour | REST requests an hour |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| Watch loop | `60N`, and `N` more at start | `60N × ceil(R / 30)`, at least `60N` |
+| Review loop | `120N` plus 120 to 240 for adoption | None |
+| Panel, while shown | About 120, unmeasured | None |
+| Both loops at `N = 10` | About 1,920 to 2,040 | At least 600 |
+| Both loops at `N = 20` | About 3,720 to 3,840 | At least 1,200 |
+
+After the change, at the same interval, with a full pass every 10 minutes,
+`C` changes an hour across the tracked pull requests, and `B` pushes an hour
+to a base that `F` fixed pull requests share:
+
+| Item | GraphQL points an hour | Counted REST requests an hour |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| Quiet passes | 0 | 0 |
+| Full passes | `12N` | `6N × ceil(R / 100)` for reviews |
+| Changes | About `2C`, more when mergeability settles a pass later | About `C`, plus the reviews read on a change |
+| Base pushes | `2BF` | `B` |
+| Adoption | 0 | One per changed list page |
+
+At `N = 10`, `C = 20`, `B = 5`, and `F = 5`, the loop spends about 210
+points an hour, against about 2,000 today. A further session on the same
+machine, on any model, adds nothing for the pull requests it shares with the
+first, because it reads the shared cache. A loop on another machine adds
+about 210 more, where a further review loop adds about 1,400 today.
+
+The loop sends `2N + 2` conditional requests a pass, about 1,300 an hour at
+`N = 10`. A `304` does not count against the primary limit. Whether it
+counts toward GitHub's secondary limits is untested. GitHub documents a
+secondary limit of 900 points a minute for REST, where a `GET` costs 1, and
+the loop sends about 22 requests a minute at `N = 10`. Halving the interval
+to 30 seconds doubles the conditional requests and adds no GraphQL on a
+quiet pass, so a new finding surfaces within about 30 seconds rather than
+within the 10 minutes a slowed watcher allows.
+
+The panel's own query is unchanged, at about 120 points an hour per session
+while shown, whatever `N` is. § 7 defers it.
+
+These figures count only polling. Each reviewer and fixer also spends
+GraphQL, through `project review census` and `publish`, summaries, and
+`gh pr view`, and the shared quota has run out in the middle of a review.
+That spend grows with the number of heads reviewed and fixed, not with the
+interval, and this plan leaves it unchanged and unmeasured. So the loop's
+210 points are a floor on the hour's total, not the total, and the
+back-off that § 7 defers protects the subagents' calls too.
+
+## 6. Rollout
+
+1. **Measure the inferences first.** On a scratch pull request in a
+   repository you own, test whether each of these moves the pull request's
+   `ETag`: resolving a thread without a reply, a body-only `COMMENT`
+   review, and a push to the base. Test whether a conditional request makes
+   GitHub recompute `mergeable`, and whether `304` answers count toward the
+   secondary limits. Record each result in § 8. Remove the reviews request
+   or the base request only where a result shows it redundant, and raise
+   `--full-every` only if resolving a thread moves the `ETag`.
+2. **Ship the change in one release.** The skill calls `project loop watch`,
+   and the CLI and both plugins share one version. When the change is too
+   large to review as one pull request, open it as a stack: the CLI command
+   and its tests, then the skill, its callers, the docs, and the packaging
+   tests, then the panel.
+3. **Let running loops end on their own.** After the upgrade, a session
+   keeps its old watcher until it restarts its loop, and the panel lists the
+   old watcher's file by its kind meanwhile. A watch loop's tracked file
+   carries over as is. A review loop's file does not: the new holder adopts
+   its pull requests again from GitHub on its first pass, so a pull request
+   dismissed from the old review loop returns until dismissed again.
+
+## 7. Open questions
+
+- **Where the loop lives.** Owner, before implementation. This plan puts it
+  in the CLI, as `project loop watch`, for the reasons in § 3. The
+  alternative is one bash script in `skills/start-pr-loop/scripts/`, which
+  avoids version skew but repeats the tracked-file parser and the review
+  markers in bash 3.2 and still calls the CLI for the lease.
+- **Whether every loop competes for the review role.** Owner, before
+  implementation. This plan says yes. The alternative is that only a loop
+  the user starts reviews, and a loop `implement` starts only fixes, which
+  keeps reviewing out of an implementing session at the cost of a dedicated
+  review session.
+- **How two machines share the review role.** Owner, before
+  implementation. This plan relies on the per-head guards and
+  `loop.review`. A lease on GitHub is deferred until two machines collide
+  often enough to need it.
+- **The skill's name.** Owner, before implementation: `start-pr-loop`.
+- **The inferences in § 2.2.** Deferred to rollout step 1. The design holds
+  whichever way each one comes out, and the results only tune
+  `--full-every` and remove requests.
+- **The panel's own query.** Deferred. It costs a constant 120 points an
+  hour per session, whatever `N` is. A follow-up can let the panel read the
+  loop's cache instead.
+- **Backing off when GraphQL runs out.** Deferred. A rate-limited query
+  fails like any other, and the loop carries the rows forward. A loop that
+  waits for the reset and says once why findings are late needs the reset
+  time from GitHub's answer.
+
+## 8. Background
+
+What each loop spends today was measured with `rateLimit { cost }` and
+`GH_DEBUG=api`, against two open pull requests in this repository, with
+`gh` 2.97.0:
+
+- **`watch-threads.sh`** spends 1 GraphQL point per pull request per pass
+  (`fetch_pr`, line 178). It reads reviews with
+  `gh api repos/<repo>/pulls/<n>/reviews --paginate` and no `per_page`
+  (`fetch_reviews`, line 214), which is `ceil(R / 30)` requests. Every reply
+  inside a thread files a review, so `R` grows over a pull request's life.
+  The check before the first pass spends one more point per pull request
+  (lines 234 to 248). The default interval is 60 seconds, with a floor of
+  30.
+- **`watch-prs.sh`** adopts with two `gh pr list` calls per pass (`listed`,
+  lines 273 to 278). Each is a GraphQL search costing 1 point, plus a
+  `SearchType` introspection costing 1 point unless `gh`'s local cache
+  answers. It spends 2 points per pull request per pass (`fetch_pr`, line
+  254) and makes no REST call.
+- **The panel** sends one batched GraphQL query every 30 seconds while it is
+  shown (`POLL_MS` in `hooks/register.tsx`). This was not measured.
+
+How `gh api` handles a conditional request, as observed:
+
+- A `200` carries a weak `ETag` (`W/"…"`), a `Last-Modified` equal to the
+  pull request's `updated_at`, and `Cache-Control: private, max-age=60`.
+- A `304` exits `1`, as a `404`, a server error, and a network failure do.
+  Only the status line that `-i` prints, `HTTP/2.0 304 Not Modified`, tells
+  them apart. Without `-i`, a `304` prints `gh: HTTP 304` to standard error.
+- `-i --jq` on a `200` prints the headers, a blank line, and then the `jq`
+  output.
+- A `304` echoes the `ETag` in its strong form, and either form gets a
+  `304`.
+
+What the REST representations hold, as observed: `GET pulls/{n}` has
+`mergeable`, `mergeable_state`, `comments`, `review_comments`, `commits`,
+`head.sha`, `draft`, `state`, `merged`, and `updated_at`. The open-pulls
+list lacks `mergeable`, `mergeable_state`, and the counts, and has `head`,
+`draft`, `updated_at`, `user`, `requested_reviewers`, and
+`requested_teams`. `requested_teams` is separate, which matches the review
+loop's rule that only a request to the login itself adopts a pull request.
+An issue comment was observed to move `updated_at`. Every other entry in the
+`Status` column of § 2.2 marked inferred was not triggered.
+
+Both manifests, `.claude-plugin/plugin.json` and `.codex-plugin/plugin.json`,
+point at `./skills/`, and `tests/test_packaging.py` checks that the
+directories holding a `SKILL.md` are exactly the published set. The CLI on
+one machine was observed at 0.7.20 while its plugin was at 0.8.0, which is
+the version skew § 3 accepts. `tests/test_watchers.py` holds 50 tests
+through a fake `gh` that refuses any argument it does not know, `-i` and
+`-H` among them.
